* text=auto

*.java text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.png binary
*.jar binary
*.jad text eol=crlf

# Vendored upstream files are verified byte-for-byte against the sha256 pins in
# tools/sdk.lock.json, so nothing may rewrite them on the way into or out of
# git. The committed copies had already drifted from upstream by 71 bytes of
# trailing whitespace, which made bootstrap.ps1 fail its own hash check on every
# fresh clone, and `* text=auto` above would have rewritten the CRLF endings too
# on the next commit. -text pins the bytes; the pin is the provenance claim.
/third_party/bc/BigInteger.java.orig -text
/third_party/bc/LICENSE.html -text

# The pinned schema sources are verified byte-for-byte the same way: their
# sha256 values are recorded in schema/UPSTREAM.md and asserted by
# tools/check-schema-drift.py and tools/tests. `* text=auto` re-writes api.tl
# to CRLF on a fresh Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true is the runner
# default), which is exactly how the layer-225 provenance tests fail in CI
# while passing against a locally generated LF working tree.
/schema/api.tl -text
/schema/api.json -text
/schema/mtproto.json -text
